; ======================================================================
;  x509.pi4 - minimal X.509 certificate-chain validation, ON THE
;             RASPBERRY PI 4 (BCM2711, AArch64)
; ======================================================================
;
;  Translated from BearSSL (c) 2016 Thomas Pornin - MIT licence.
;  The notice ships as licenses/BearSSL-LICENSE.txt (release condition).
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/x509.pi4"
;
;  This is the HOST half of the "minimal" X.509 engine. The parsing,
;  chain-walk, OID matching, validity comparison ordering and extension
;  handling all live in AHEAD-OF-TIME threaded bytecode (the x509_blob
;  include, lifted from the engine's T0 source by tools/t0gen.py). This
;  file supplies the native words that bytecode calls out to, plus a
;  driver that feeds a chain through it and reads back a verdict.
;
;  X.509 IS PUBLIC DATA. Certificates travel on the wire in the clear, so
;  nothing here is constant-time and nothing should be - there is no
;  secret to leak while parsing a certificate. (There is NO certificate-
;  transparency requirement in scope either; CT is a policy layer above
;  chain validation and is out of this module.) The secret-bearing work -
;  the modular exponent for RSA, the point multiply for ECDSA - is reached
;  ONLY through the signature-verify seam below and carries its own
;  constant-time discipline in its own module.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE VERDICT IS A CODE. IT IS NOT A FLAG AND IT IS NOT A MASK.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  #X509ERR_OK IS 32. Test the result of X509ValidateChain as
;  `= #X509ERR_OK`, never as "non-zero" (32 is non-zero and so is 62),
;  never as `= 1`, and never as `= $FFFFFFFF`.
;
;  THIS CRYPTO FAMILY NOW HAS THREE VERDICT CONVENTIONS ON THIS PART and
;  mixing them is a trap, so all three are written here:
;
;      GcmDecrypt     a MASK      test `= $FFFFFFFF`
;      EcP256Mul      a FLAG      test `= 1`
;      EcdsaVrfy      a FLAG      1 ok / 0 bad / -1 not linked
;      X509Validate*  a CODE      test `= #X509ERR_OK` (32)
;
;  The reason this matters more here than anywhere else is that a
;  validator that answers the WRONG NON-OK CODE still looks like a
;  working validator to a caller that only asks "did it fail". The gate
;  for this file therefore checks the NAME of every refusal, not merely
;  that a refusal happened - see tools/a64/a64_x509_check.py.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE PUBLIC API
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;    X509ResetAnchors()
;        forget all configured trust anchors.
;
;    X509AddAnchorRSA(isCA, dnAddr, dnLen, nAddr, nLen, eAddr, eLen)
;    X509AddAnchorEC (isCA, dnAddr, dnLen, curve, qAddr, qLen)
;        register a trust anchor. isCA<>0 marks a CA anchor (used to sign
;        the chain); isCA=0 marks a directly-trusted end-entity key (key
;        pinning - matched by DN hash + public key, no signature needed).
;
;    X509ValidateChain(certsAddr, lensAddr, count, serverAddr, serverLen,
;                      unixTime)
;        Validate a chain of `count` DER certificates. certsAddr points at
;        an array of `count` cell-sized addresses (EE first); lensAddr at a
;        matching array of lengths. serverAddr/serverLen give the expected
;        server name (serverLen<0 => no name check). unixTime is the
;        seconds-since-1970 date to check validity against. Returns the
;        engine verdict code in X509Err (see #X509ERR_*): #X509ERR_OK (32)
;        means validated. On success the decoded end-entity public key is
;        available through X509EePkeyType and the X509EePkey* globals.
;
;  > THE CALLER'S ARRAY-OF-CELLS CONTRACT, ON THIS PART.  certsAddr and
;  > lensAddr point at arrays of `.i` CELLS - `Dim gC.i[n]` - and A CELL
;  > IS EIGHT BYTES HERE.  The stride is #X_CELL and the read is PeekI,
;  > which reads eight.  Do NOT hand this procedure a `.l` or `.n` array
;  > and do not compute the addresses yourself: `@gC` is the base and the
;  > library does the arithmetic.  This is the line the port got wrong -
;  > see SHAPE 1 below.
;
;  THE SIGNATURE-VERIFY SEAM (module M9 ecdsa / M10 rsa wire in here).
;  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Signature checks call two seam procedures. They are NOT implemented
;  here (a library never includes another library). The MAIN program must
;  define #X509_SIG_LINKED before including this file: 0 means the seam is
;  stubbed here (the loud stubs below answer "not linked" with -1, and any
;  chain that reaches a signature check ends with #X509ERR_SEAM (200) so the
;  gap is impossible to miss); 1 means the main has already included the
;  ecdsa and rsa modules - which define RsaVrfy / EcdsaVrfy - ahead of this
;  file, so the stubs are compiled out and real verification runs.
;
;    RsaSetPubKey(nAddr, nLen, eAddr, eLen)
;        install the public key the next RsaVrfy uses.
;    RsaVrfy(sigAddr, sigLen, hashOidAddr, hashLen, outAddr)
;                                        -> 1 ok / 0 bad / -1 unlinked
;        PKCS#1 v1.5 verify: recover the signed hash into outAddr (hashLen
;        bytes); the caller compares it against the computed TBS hash.
;
;    EcdsaVrfy(curve, hashAddr, hashLen, qAddr, qLen, sigAddr, sigLen)
;                                        -> 1 ok / 0 bad / -1 unlinked
;        ECDSA verify of the DER signature over the hash at hashAddr.
;
;  > THE RSA SEAM IS SPLIT HERE, AND IT IS ARITY AND NOT WIDTH THAT DID
;  > IT.  The 32-bit families declare ONE nine-argument RsaVrfy.  This
;  > backend passes EIGHT and refuses a ninth with a FRONTEND FATAL
;  > ERROR - not a warning, so the portable spelling does not compile on
;  > this part at all, neither the declaration nor the call.  The four
;  > public-key arguments therefore move into an added RsaSetPubKey,
;  > which is EXACTLY the split RaspberryPi4/Lib/rsa.pi4 already made
;  > against the same wall and already exports.  The payoff is that the
;  > RSA half of the seam is LINKABLE on this part, where the .pico2
;  > proof has to keep it stubbed.  One addition and one same-name
;  > signature change, both declared in tools/check_tls_twins.py.
;
;  REQUIRES (the MAIN file lists these, in order, before this one):
;    * t0vm      - the bytecode VM this engine runs on.
;    * sha256    - the TBS hash and the DN hash are both SHA-256 here.
;                  ON THIS PART THAT FILE IS Anvil/Core/sha256.pbi, not
;                  RaspberryPi4/Lib/sha256.pi4: it moved into the shared
;                  Anvil core when Anvil became a real bootloader and the
;                  Arduino UNO Q includes it from there.
;    * x509_blob - the lifted engine bytecode (defines T0LoadX509 and the
;                  #T0N_* native ids serviced below).
;    * ecdsa, rsa (optional) - the signature-verify seam. Absent => stub.
;  It includes none of them itself (house rule: a library never includes a
;  library). It also turns EnableExplicit on for your program.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   .i IS 64 BITS HERE.  THE FOUR-SHAPE AUDIT, PLUS THE FIFTH
;  ======================================================================
;
;  The ways a 32-bit portable core breaks on this part were measured in
;  Anvil/Core/sha256.pbi, RaspberryPi4/Lib/gcm.pi4,
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/bignum.pi4 and RaspberryPi4/Lib/t0vm.pi4.  Every one
;  was looked for here and the finding is recorded either way - "not
;  present" is a result, not a silence.
;
;  SHAPE 1 - AN ARRAY ELEMENT IS EIGHT BYTES, NOT FOUR.
;      PRESENT, AND IT IS THE ONE REAL DEFECT THIS PORT FOUND.  The
;      distinction that matters is INDEXED ACCESS versus MANUAL ADDRESS
;      ARITHMETIC, and this file has both.
;
;      SAFE - the compiler scales the index, so the eight-byte element
;      costs memory and nothing else:
;        * the eleven anchor tables `Dim xAnc*.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]` are
;          only ever read as `xAncCA[u]`, `xAncDnAddr[u]` and so on.
;          Eleven arrays x 6 entries x 8 bytes = 528 bytes here against
;          264 on a Pico.  That is the whole cost.
;        * every other array in the file - xRam, xTbsSnap, xDnSnap,
;          xTbsHash, xHashByte, xTmpHash, xEePkey - is `.a`, ONE byte
;          per element on every family, and `@xRam[#X_pkey_data + nlen]`
;          / `@xEePkey[nlen]` are indexed address-of on a `.a` array and
;          scale by one.
;
;      THE DEFECT - X509ValidateChain read the caller's certificate
;      array with MANUAL arithmetic:
;
;          certAddr = PeekI(certsAddr + i * 4)   ; cell-sized array
;          certLen  = PeekI(lensAddr + i * 4)    ; (32-bit targets)
;
;      and the comment says "32-bit targets" out loud.  BOTH HALVES ARE
;      WRONG HERE AND THEY ARE WRONG SEPARATELY: PeekI reads EIGHT bytes
;      on this part, and the stride 4 is half a cell.  Element 0 happens
;      to come back right; from element 1 onwards the read straddles the
;      high half of one cell and the low half of the next, so a
;      TWO-CERTIFICATE CHAIN reads a garbage address and a garbage
;      length for its issuer.  A one-certificate chain never notices,
;      which is exactly why the gate for this file carries
;      multi-certificate chains and why mutation M1 restores the stride
;      and must die.
;
;      Fixed by naming the contract instead of hiding it: #X_CELL is the
;      caller's cell size and the stride and the read width are both
;      that.  The contract is written into the API block above, because
;      the shape lands on the CALLER as much as on this file - a caller
;      that declares `Dim gC.l[8]` compiles and is wrong.
;
;      THE CONTEXT-RAM OFFSETS ARE FINE, and every one was checked
;      rather than assumed.  #X_key_usages(0) #X_cert_signer_key_type(1)
;      #X_cert_sig_hash_len(2) #X_cert_sig_hash_oid(4) #X_cert_sig_len(6)
;      #X_min_rsa_size(8) #X_cert_length(12) #X_num_certs(16)
;      #X_current_dn_hash(32) #X_next_dn_hash(96) #X_saved_dn_hash(160)
;      #X_pad(224) #X_pkey_data(480) #X_cert_sig(1000) are BYTE offsets
;      into a `.a` array and are unchanged by the word size; the
;      declared regions abut without overlapping (224+256=480,
;      480+520=1000, 1000+512=1512) and the last ends inside
;      #X_RAM_SIZE(2048).  They MUST also stay equal to the
;      field=offset map t0gen.py was given when the blob was lifted, and
;      that map is what the blob's own get/set words encode - so an
;      "improvement" here silently rewrites the bytecode's idea of where
;      the signature lives.
;
;  SHAPE 2 - PeekL SIGN-EXTENDS, AND SO DOES PeekB.
;      NOT PRESENT.  There is no PeekL, no PeekB, no PokeL and no Read.b
;      in this file.  DER PARSING IS ALL BYTE READS and every one of
;      them is `PeekA(...) & 255` or a read of the `.a` context array;
;      the 32-bit copies were already written that way, because a length
;      byte of $80 read signed is not 128 on a Cortex-M0+ either.  The
;      only Peek that is not a byte read is the PeekI of SHAPE 1, and it
;      is deliberate: it reads a whole cell.
;
;  SHAPE 3 - `<<` DOES NOT DISCARD AT BIT 31.
;      PRESENT IN EXACTLY ONE PLACE, Xr32's four-byte assembly:
;
;          (b0) | (b1 << 8) | (b2 << 16) | (b3 << 24)
;
;      On a 32-bit `.i` a top byte of $80 or more lands in the sign bit
;      and the result is NEGATIVE; here it does not and the result is
;      the positive value 0..$FFFFFFFF.  For the file's own use that is
;      strictly better - #X_cert_length and #X_num_certs are unsigned
;      quantities and Xr32 now returns them unsigned - and
;      XrSet32/XrSet16/XrSet8 mask every byte on the way out, so a
;      negative cell stores correctly either way.
;
;      IT IS NOT BETTER AT ONE PLACE: the boundary where a host value
;      becomes a T0 CELL.  t0vm.pi4's contract is that a cell holds the
;      SIGN-EXTENDED int32 value, so -1 is $FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and never
;      $00000000FFFFFFFF, and a native word pushing something wider than
;      31 bits must push T0Norm(v).  XnMem's get32 is the ONLY native in
;      this file that can push such a value, so it is the only one
;      wrapped - which is precisely what t0vm.pi4's own #T0OP_GET32 does
;      one file over.  Every other push here is a byte, a small count, a
;      verdict code, 0 or -1, and all of those are already canonical.
;      Mutation M3 removes the wrapper and the gate drives XnMem
;      directly to watch it die at full 64-bit width.
;
;  SHAPE 4 - A 32-BIT COUNTER NEVER WRAPS.
;      NOT PRESENT.  The only counter in the file is X509RunOneCert's
;      `guard`, an `.i` that is compared against a literal 20000000 and
;      is not allowed to wrap by design - it exists to stop a runaway
;      bytecode program, and a wider register makes it strictly harder
;      to fool.  #X_num_certs is a u32 in the context RAM, but
;      XrSet32 truncates to four bytes by construction and a chain has
;      single digits of certificates in it.
;
;  ---- THE FIFTH, THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY BITES --------------------------
;
;  `>> 31` AS A SIGN TEST AND `x ! $FFFFFFFF` AS A LOGICAL NOT.
;      NOT PRESENT, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT: the file contains NO `!`
;      operator at all, no `$FFFFFFFF`, and no right shift other than
;      the three `>> 8 / >> 16 / >> 24` inside XrSet32 that immediately
;      mask with `& 255`.  Every decision in this engine is spelled as
;      an ordinary `If`, and every verdict it produces is a small
;      LITERAL CODE assigned to xErr - never a mask built out of a sign
;      bit.  That is why the file that decides whether to trust a
;      stranger's certificate needed no rewriting for the shape that
;      made GcmDecrypt accept a forged tag.
;
;      SAYING SO IS NOT ENOUGH, because there is no diff for a reviewer
;      to read.  tools/a64/a64_x509_check.py therefore PLANTS the shape:
;      mutations M4 and M5 respell X509VerifySig's two "did the
;      signature verify" tests and X509RunOneCert's terminal test in the
;      GcmDecrypt form, and the sweep records what happens.  The audit
;      is swept, not asserted.
;
;  ---- ONE MEASURED BEHAVIOURAL DIFFERENCE THAT IS NOT A SHAPE --------
;
;  THE VALIDITY DATE ARITHMETIC REACHES A DIFFERENT BRANCH HERE, and it
;  is worth writing down because it means this part tests MORE than the
;  32-bit families do rather than less.  The shared gate's "expired"
;  case passes unixTime = 4070908800 (2099-01-01), which does not fit a
;  signed 32-bit integer: on the Picos it arrives negative, the day
;  number lands in 1963, and the case is refused by the notBefore branch
;  of check-validity-range.  Here the literal is exact, the day number
;  is 766645, and the case is refused by the notAfter branch - the one
;  the case is named after.  Both answer #X509ERR_EXPIRED (54); only on
;  this part do the "expired" and "notBefore" cases exercise the two
;  different sides of the comparison.
;
;  ======================================================================
;   WHAT IS *NOT* DIFFERENT
;  ======================================================================
;
;  THE WHOLE 64-BIT DIFF IS TWO LINES AND A CONSTANT: the cell stride,
;  the T0Norm at the get32 push, and #X_CELL naming the first.  Nothing
;  else in this file changed for the word size, and the reason is where
;  the width lives - this file is byte handling, `.a` array indexing and
;  calls into t0vm and sha256, and the two modules that DO compute on
;  32-bit words paid for the width once each in their own file.
;
;  The RSA seam split is the OTHER divergence and it is not a 64-bit
;  question at all; it is the eight-argument calling convention, and it
;  is argued in the seam block above.
;
;  x509_blob.pi4 is PURE DATA and is byte-identical, below its header,
;  to RP2350/Lib/x509_blob.pico2. It is not written, it is lifted; see
;  that file's header for how the .pi4 copy was produced and what gates
;  it.
;
;  PROOF STATUS (Raspberry Pi 4 / Cortex-A72, AArch64): builds clean for
;  pi4; runs the lifted engine bytecode on the landed t0vm.pi4 over the
;  SAME real captured certificate chains the three 32-bit families run
;  (tools/a64/a64_x509_check.py parses them out of tools/x509_proof_gen.py
;  and tools/ecdsa_x509_gen.py rather than carrying a copy), under the
;  A64 model.  EVERY POSITION OF THE SEAM SWITCH IS RUN:
;    * MODE A, #X509_SIG_LINKED = 0.  Every non-signature verdict is
;      produced BY NAME - direct-trust EC and RSA validate (32), expired
;      (54), notBefore (54), bad server name (56), DN mismatch on a
;      broken two-certificate chain (55), untrusted root (62), empty
;      chain (35) - and both real CA chains stop at #X509ERR_SEAM (200),
;      never at OK.
;    * MODE B, #X509_SIG_LINKED = 1 with ecdsa.pi4 linked and the RSA
;      half locally stubbed, which is exactly the image
;      ecdsaX509Proof.pico2 builds.  The real EC chain that stopped at
;      200 comes back #X509ERR_OK (32); a flipped signature byte on the
;      end-entity, a flipped byte on the intermediate and a malformed
;      signature encoding each come back #X509ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE (52);
;      the RSA chain still reports 200.
;    * MODE C, #X509_SIG_LINKED = 1 with BOTH ecdsa.pi4 and rsa.pi4
;      linked - which no 32-bit family can build, because the split
;      above is what makes the RSA seam satisfiable.  The RSA chain
;      that answered 200 in modes A and B comes back #X509ERR_OK (32),
;      and the same tamper applied to an RSA signature comes back 52.
;  Mutation-swept under that gate's --mutate, and structurally twinned
;  under tools/check_tls_twins.py --cross x509.
;  NOTHING HERE HAS RUN ON SILICON.
;
;  ONLY SHA-256 signature/DN hashing is wired (the landed hash). A chain
;  signed under SHA-1/384/512 is reported UNSUPPORTED, loudly, not guessed.
;
;  CONTEXT RAM IS BOUNDS-CHECKED HERE, NOT IN THE VM. This engine owns its
;  own context array because it was lifted with --native-ram, which takes
;  the six memory words out of the VM - and out of reach of the VM's own
;  bounds guard. Every offset that arrives from the T0 data stack is
;  width-checked by XrInRange below; out of range is #X509ERR_RAM_BOUNDS
;  (900) and a halted machine, never a wrapped read or a neighbour write.
;
;  THE KEY BUFFER IS A SECOND BOUND, NOT THE SAME ONE. The decoded
;  end-entity key is copied out of that array into xEePkey, which is a
;  quarter of its size, and the copy's length is a stack value like any
;  offset. XrInRange cannot see that write at all, so XkeyFits guards it
;  (and the two verify natives that hand the same lengths out as a raw
;  address) with its own named refusal, #X509ERR_KEY_BOUNDS (901).
;
;  THE GUARD'S FORM IS WHAT MAKES IT WIDTH-INDEPENDENT, and that is the
;  reason this copy takes the 32-bit copies' spelling instead of a
;  re-derivation of it. `n > SIZE - e` is correct at every width;
;  `n + e > SIZE` is correct at none. The 32-bit probe's wrap case - two
;  $7FFFFFFF halves whose naive sum lands at -2 - does NOT reproduce here,
;  because `.i` is 64 bits and those two halves sum to 4,294,967,294 with
;  room to spare. That is not the guard being unnecessary; it is the
;  hostile pair having moved. The Pi 4 probe uses $7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, which
;  wraps this part's arithmetic exactly as the 32-bit pair wraps theirs,
;  and the naive test is run beside the real one so the report shows it
;  answering "fits" on the line above the one that refuses.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit
; ==== PORTABLE CORE A64 BEGIN ====

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Verdict codes (BearSSL BR_ERR_X509_*), plus three of our own.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#X509ERR_OK                 = 32
#X509ERR_UNSUPPORTED        = 49
#X509ERR_WRONG_KEY_TYPE     = 51
#X509ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE      = 52
#X509ERR_TIME_UNKNOWN       = 53
#X509ERR_EXPIRED            = 54
#X509ERR_DN_MISMATCH        = 55
#X509ERR_BAD_SERVER_NAME    = 56
#X509ERR_NOT_CA             = 58
#X509ERR_WEAK_PUBLIC_KEY    = 60
#X509ERR_NOT_TRUSTED        = 62
#X509ERR_EMPTY_CHAIN        = 35
#X509ERR_SEAM               = 200   ; signature seam not linked (M9/M10)
#X509ERR_GUARD              = 199   ; run guard tripped (should never fire)
; Same number, same meaning, same shape as the t0vm refusal of the same
; name: a context-RAM offset outside the region. Whichever engine owns the
; array, a 900 in a log means exactly one thing.
#X509ERR_RAM_BOUNDS         = 900   ; context-RAM offset out of range
; A SECOND bound, over a DIFFERENT array. 900 is the context RAM; 901 is
; the permanent end-entity key buffer, which is a quarter of its size and
; is not covered by XrInRange at all. Two arrays, two numbers, so a log
; line says which one was overrun.
#X509ERR_KEY_BOUNDS         = 901   ; decoded key longer than xEePkey

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Context-RAM layout. These offsets MUST match the field=offset map given
;  to tools/t0gen.py when x509_blob was lifted: the bytecode's get/set
;  words and the native words below both address this one flat array, so a
;  single layout serves both. The engine's key/signature/scratch buffers
;  far exceed the t0vm's own small t0_ram, which is why the six memory ops
;  were lifted as natives (--native-ram) onto this host-owned array.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#X_key_usages           = 0     ; u8
#X_cert_signer_key_type = 1     ; u8
#X_cert_sig_hash_len    = 2     ; u8
#X_cert_sig_hash_oid    = 4     ; u16 (offset into the DATA block)
#X_cert_sig_len         = 6     ; u16
#X_min_rsa_size         = 8     ; u16 (min modulus bytes, minus 128)
#X_cert_length          = 12    ; u32
#X_num_certs            = 16    ; u32
#X_current_dn_hash      = 32    ; 32 bytes (subject DN hash)
#X_next_dn_hash         = 96    ; 32 bytes (issuer DN hash, this cert)
#X_saved_dn_hash        = 160   ; 32 bytes (issuer DN hash, carried)
#X_pad                  = 224   ; 256 bytes (OID / name / value scratch)
#X_pkey_data            = 480   ; 520 bytes (currently decoded key)
#X_cert_sig             = 1000  ; 512 bytes (saved signature)
#X_RAM_SIZE             = 2048

#X_DNHASH_LEN = 32              ; SHA-256 digest length

; THE CALLER'S ARRAY-OF-CELLS STRIDE.  X509ValidateChain is handed
; two arrays of `.i` cells - `Dim gC.i[n]`, `@gC` as the base - and
; ON THIS PART A CELL IS EIGHT BYTES.  The 32-bit copies hard-code 4
; at the read site with the comment "32-bit targets"; naming it is
; how the contract stops being a comment.  PeekI reads exactly this
; many bytes here, so the stride and the access width are the same
; number and cannot drift apart.  SizeOf(Integer) is not accepted in
; a constant declaration by this compiler (measured: SYNTAX ERROR,
; Invalid constant declaration), so it is written out.
#X_CELL = 8

Global Dim xRam.a[#X_RAM_SIZE]

; ---- run state / verdict ----
Global X509Err.i               ; verdict, read by the caller and the gate
Global xErr.i                  ; engine err set by fail / trust natives

; ---- input feed (the "hbuf/hlen" of the C driver) ----
Global xHbuf.i
Global xHlen.i

; ---- hashing: two running SHA-256 kept as 104-byte snapshots so the TBS
;      hash and the DN hash can both absorb the same byte stream through
;      the one landed single-context hasher ----
Global xDoMhash.i
Global xDoDnhash.i
Global Dim xTbsSnap.a[104]
Global Dim xDnSnap.a[104]
Global Dim xTbsHash.a[64]      ; finished TBS hash
Global Dim xHashByte.a[4]      ; 1-byte feed scratch (for Sha256Update)
Global Dim xTmpHash.a[64]      ; scratch for anchor DN hashing / recovered hash

; ---- expected server name ----
Global xServerAddr.i
Global xServerLen.i            ; <0 means "no server name expected"

; ---- validity date, in the engine's day/second epoch ----
Global xValidDays.i
Global xValidSecs.i

; ---- decoded end-entity public key (result of a validated chain) ----
Global X509EePkeyType.i        ; 1=RSA, 2=EC, 0=none
Global X509EeNAddr.i
Global X509EeNLen.i
Global X509EeEAddr.i
Global X509EeELen.i
Global X509EeCurve.i
Global X509EeQAddr.i
Global X509EeQLen.i
; The permanent EE key buffer, and its capacity as a NAMED constant so the
; array and the bound that protects it are one fact and cannot drift. It
; matches the 520-byte #X_pkey_data window on purpose - the copy below
; moves one into the other - but they are separate arrays and each needs
; its own guard. See XkeyFits.
#X_EEPKEY_SIZE = 520
Global Dim xEePkey.a[#X_EEPKEY_SIZE]   ; permanent EE key bytes

; ---- currently-decoded key type (for direct trust / verify) ----
Global xPkeyType.i

; ---- trust anchors ----
#X_MAX_ANCHORS = 6
Global xNumAnchors.i
Global Dim xAncCA.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncDnAddr.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncDnLen.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncType.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncNAddr.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncNLen.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncEAddr.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncELen.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncCurve.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncQAddr.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]
Global Dim xAncQLen.i[#X_MAX_ANCHORS]

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CONTEXT-RAM BOUNDS - the one check that stands between a hostile input
;  and the globals next door.
;
;  This engine was lifted with --native-ram, so the six context-memory
;  words are NOT executed by the t0vm and never meet its T0RamInRange:
;  they arrive here as native words, with the offset popped straight off
;  the T0 data stack. A T0 program COMPUTES those offsets out of the bytes
;  it is parsing, and the bytes THIS engine parses are certificates handed
;  over by a stranger. A bare indexed access into xRam therefore lets a
;  computed offset read - or WRITE - whatever global happens to sit beside
;  the array, and answer as if nothing had happened. That is the
;  silent-wrong failure this project refuses on principle.
;
;  So every xRam touch reached from the data stack goes through XrInRange
;  first, and an offset outside the region is a LOUD, NAMED refusal:
;  #X509ERR_RAM_BOUNDS lands in xErr and the machine halts (t0_ip = -1),
;  so the driver's very next xErr test returns terminal and the caller
;  sees the code instead of a wrong answer. Same guard, same shape and the
;  same number as t0vm's, because it is the same hole - owning the array
;  privately does not make the offsets trustworthy.
;
;  n is the access width in bytes (1, 2, 4, or a blob length). The highest
;  byte touched is a + n - 1, so the last legal offset is #X_RAM_SIZE - n;
;  a 32-bit access at #X_RAM_SIZE - 1 is rejected, not truncated.
;
;  SHAPE AUDIT ON THIS PART: a and n are .i and .i is 64 bits here, so the
;  comparison is the same comparison the 32-bit copies make - there is no
;  width to wrap and no sign bit at 31 to shift down. The guard is
;  byte-for-byte the 32-bit spelling on purpose.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XrInRange(a.i, n.i)
  If a < 0 Or a > (#X_RAM_SIZE - n)
    xErr = #X509ERR_RAM_BOUNDS
    t0_ip = -1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Small context-RAM accessors (little-endian, as the engine's C uses).
;  A refused read answers 0, but xErr is already set and the machine is
;  already halted, so the 0 can never be mistaken for data.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Xr8(a.i)
  If XrInRange(a, 1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn xRam[a] & 255
EndProcedure
Procedure XrSet8(a.i, v.i)
  If XrInRange(a, 1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  xRam[a] = v & 255
EndProcedure
Procedure.i Xr16(a.i)
  If XrInRange(a, 2) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (xRam[a] & 255) | ((xRam[a + 1] & 255) << 8)
EndProcedure
Procedure XrSet16(a.i, v.i)
  If XrInRange(a, 2) = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  xRam[a] = v & 255
  xRam[a + 1] = (v >> 8) & 255
EndProcedure
Procedure.i Xr32(a.i)
  If XrInRange(a, 4) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn (xRam[a] & 255) | ((xRam[a + 1] & 255) << 8) | ((xRam[a + 2] & 255) << 16) | ((xRam[a + 3] & 255) << 24)
EndProcedure
Procedure XrSet32(a.i, v.i)
  If XrInRange(a, 4) = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  xRam[a] = v & 255
  xRam[a + 1] = (v >> 8) & 255
  xRam[a + 2] = (v >> 16) & 255
  xRam[a + 3] = (v >> 24) & 255
EndProcedure

; A raw byte compare over the context RAM. Both ends are stack-supplied,
; so both are ranged over the whole length before a single byte is read;
; a refused compare answers "not equal" with xErr already set.
Procedure.i XramEq(p1.i, p2.i, len.i)
  Protected i.i
  If XrInRange(p1, len) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XrInRange(p2, len) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  i = 0
  While i < len
    If xRam[p1 + i] <> xRam[p2 + i]
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SECOND BOUND - the permanent key buffer, which XrInRange does not
;  cover and cannot.
;
;  When a chain validates, the end-entity key is copied OUT of the context
;  RAM and INTO xEePkey so it outlives the parse. XrInRange guards the
;  SOURCE of that copy; the DESTINATION is a different array, a quarter of
;  the size, and the copy's length is popped off the T0 data stack exactly
;  like an offset is - so it is derived from certificate bytes and gets
;  the same treatment.
;
;  Reading the lifted bytecode says no certificate can drive the length
;  past 520 today, and this was disassembled rather than assumed. One word
;  pushes the key window as the literal pair (480, 520). The RSA path hands
;  that pair to the integer reader, which spends one unit of capacity per
;  byte it stores and refuses the moment the remaining capacity reaches
;  zero; the exponent is then read into the same window with whatever the
;  modulus left behind, so modulus + exponent sum to at most 520 by
;  construction. The EC path is blunter - it compares the bit-string length
;  against the same 520 before it copies a byte. Both refuse with the
;  decoder's own "value too large" verdict, 50.
;
;  Note the array holds exactly #X_EEPKEY_SIZE bytes, indices 0 to
;  #X_EEPKEY_SIZE - 1, so a pair summing to exactly the capacity fills it
;  and writes nothing beyond. That is the last legal size and it is an
;  ACCEPTANCE case in the probe, not a refusal.
;
;  That is not a reason to leave the copy unbounded. The cap lives in a
;  GENERATED data blob marked "do not edit by hand, regenerate", and the
;  window it caps at is a number that happens to equal #X_EEPKEY_SIZE -
;  nothing ties the two together and neither file mentions the other. A
;  blob regenerated from an upstream configured for larger keys (8192-bit
;  RSA is 1024 bytes of modulus alone) would overrun this buffer in
;  silence. And the native dispatch is a public entry point: whatever is
;  on the stack when it is called is what these cases use.
;
;  So the destination gets its own named refusal, #X509ERR_KEY_BOUNDS
;  (901), in XrInRange's shape - xErr set, machine halted with t0_ip = -1,
;  so the driver's next check returns terminal. The source is then ranged
;  by XrInRange as usual, which keeps the read side honest even if the
;  layout constants ever move.
;
;  The two do-vrfy natives get the same check for the same reason: they
;  take the SAME two stack lengths and turn them into a raw address into
;  xRam that leaves this file entirely, inside a pointer the verifier will
;  read. The key window at #X_pkey_data is itself #X_EEPKEY_SIZE bytes -
;  that is what the layout above says - so one constant is the honest
;  bound for all four sites; if either buffer is ever resized, this
;  constant is the thing that has to change.
;
;  n and e are the two halves of an RSA key (modulus, exponent); an EC key
;  passes its q length as n and 0 as e. Written as n > SIZE - e rather
;  than n + e > SIZE so a hostile pair cannot wrap the addition past the
;  test - the same reason XrInRange is written a > SIZE - n.
;
;  SHAPE AUDIT ON THIS PART: n and e are .i and .i is 64 bits here, so
;  this is the 32-bit spelling unchanged, deliberately. The WIDTH moved
;  and the FORM did not have to: the pair that wraps a 64-bit add is
;  larger than the pair that wraps a 32-bit one, and `n > SIZE - e` is
;  correct against both without knowing which register it is running in.
;  That is the whole argument for porting the form rather than
;  re-deriving the test.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i XkeyFits(n.i, e.i)
  If n < 0 Or e < 0 Or n > (#X_EEPKEY_SIZE - e)
    xErr = #X509ERR_KEY_BOUNDS
    t0_ip = -1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XrInRange(#X_pkey_data, n + e) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Hash feeding. Absorb one byte (read8-low) or a chunk (read-blob-inner)
;  into whichever running hashes are active, snapshotting around each use
;  of the one shared hasher.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure XfeedByte(b.i)
  xHashByte[0] = b & 255
  If xDoMhash
    Sha256Restore(@xTbsSnap)
    Sha256Update(@xHashByte, 1)
    Sha256Snapshot(@xTbsSnap)
  EndIf
  If xDoDnhash
    Sha256Restore(@xDnSnap)
    Sha256Update(@xHashByte, 1)
    Sha256Snapshot(@xDnSnap)
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure XfeedChunk(addr.i, len.i)
  If len <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If xDoMhash
    Sha256Restore(@xTbsSnap)
    Sha256Update(addr, len)
    Sha256Snapshot(@xTbsSnap)
  EndIf
  If xDoDnhash
    Sha256Restore(@xDnSnap)
    Sha256Update(addr, len)
    Sha256Snapshot(@xDnSnap)
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SIGNATURE-VERIFY SEAM. verify_signature() decides key type then
;  calls out through RsaVrfy / EcdsaVrfy. Those live in the ecdsa/rsa
;  modules when linked; the loud stubs return -1 ("not linked") otherwise.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
CompilerIf #X509_SIG_LINKED = 0
; THE NINTH ARGUMENT.  This backend passes EIGHT arguments and the
; portable seam declares NINE, so the four public-key arguments move
; into an added RsaSetPubKey - exactly the split
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/rsa.pi4 already made, for the same reason and
; against the same wall.  That module's five-argument RsaVrfy IS this
; shape: its header calls itself "the CERTIFICATE SEAM entry point"
; and promises never to return -1, so the -1 below stays
; unambiguous.  THIS IS NOT A PREFERENCE - the refusal is a FRONTEND
; FATAL ERROR, so the nine-argument spelling does not compile on this
; part at all, neither the declaration nor the call.  The payoff is
; that the RSA half of the seam is LINKABLE here, where the .pico2
; proof has to keep it stubbed.
Procedure RsaSetPubKey(nPtr.i, nLen.i, ePtr.i, eLen.i)
  ; Not yet linked (module M10). Nothing to install; the loud answer
  ; comes from RsaVrfy below.
  nPtr = 0 : nLen = 0 : ePtr = 0 : eLen = 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i RsaVrfy(sigAddr.i, sigLen.i, hashOidAddr.i, hashLen.i, outAddr.i)
  ; Not yet linked (module M10). Loud: -1 => #X509ERR_SEAM at the caller.
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
Procedure.i EcdsaVrfy(curve.i, hashAddr.i, hashLen.i, qAddr.i, qLen.i, sigAddr.i, sigLen.i)
  ; Not yet linked (module M9). Loud: -1 => #X509ERR_SEAM at the caller.
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
CompilerEndIf

; verify_signature(pkType, nOrCurve, ...) against the saved TBS hash and
; the saved signature. Returns 0 on success, or a non-zero verdict code.
;   For RSA: keyN/keyNlen/keyE/keyElen give the modulus and exponent.
;   For EC:  keyN=curve, keyE=q address, keyElen=q length.
Procedure.i X509VerifySig(pkType.i, keyN.i, keyNlen.i, keyE.i, keyElen.i)
  Protected r.i
  Protected sigAddr.i
  Protected sigLen.i
  Protected hashLen.i
  Protected hashOidAddr.i

  If pkType <> Xr8(#X_cert_signer_key_type)
    ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_WRONG_KEY_TYPE
  EndIf

  sigAddr = @xRam[#X_cert_sig]
  sigLen  = Xr16(#X_cert_sig_len)
  hashLen = Xr8(#X_cert_sig_hash_len)
  hashOidAddr = t0_datap + Xr16(#X_cert_sig_hash_oid)

  If pkType = 1                       ; RSA
    ; THE NINTH ARGUMENT - install the key, then verify. See the seam
    ; block above; rsa.pi4 exports exactly this pair.
    RsaSetPubKey(keyN, keyNlen, keyE, keyElen)
    r = RsaVrfy(sigAddr, sigLen, hashOidAddr, hashLen, @xTmpHash)
    If r < 0
      ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_SEAM
    EndIf
    If r = 0
      ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE
    EndIf
    ; recovered hash must equal the computed TBS hash
    Protected i.i
    i = 0
    While i < hashLen
      If (xTmpHash[i] & 255) <> (xTbsHash[i] & 255)
        ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE
      EndIf
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    ProcedureReturn 0
  ElseIf pkType = 2                   ; EC
    r = EcdsaVrfy(keyN, @xTbsHash, hashLen, keyE, keyElen, sigAddr, sigLen)
    If r < 0
      ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_SEAM
    EndIf
    If r = 0
      ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #X509ERR_UNSUPPORTED
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Native-word groups. Split into small procedures so no dispatch branch
;  reaches too far for the RA4M1 assembler's relaxation (the same reason
;  t0vm splits its primitive groups).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; get16/get32/set8/set16/set32 (the context memory ops lifted as natives).
Procedure XnMem(nid.i)
  Protected a.i
  Protected v.i
  Select nid
    Case #T0N_set8
      a = T0Pop()
      v = T0Pop()
      XrSet8(a, v)
    Case #T0N_set16
      a = T0Pop()
      v = T0Pop()
      XrSet16(a, v)
    Case #T0N_set32
      a = T0Pop()
      v = T0Pop()
      XrSet32(a, v)
    Case #T0N_get16
      a = T0Pop()
      T0Push(Xr16(a))
    Case #T0N_get32
      a = T0Pop()
      ; SHAPE 3 AT THE ONE PLACE IT MATTERS.  Xr32's `<< 24` does not
      ; discard at bit 31 here, so a top byte of $80 or more comes
      ; back as a POSITIVE value up to $FFFFFFFF.  That is the right
      ; answer for this file's own use and the WRONG SHAPE for a T0
      ; cell: t0vm.pi4 defines a cell as the SIGN-EXTENDED int32, so
      ; -1 must be $FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and never $00000000FFFFFFFF, and
      ; its contract says a native pushing more than 31 bits pushes
      ; T0Norm(v).  t0vm.pi4's own #T0OP_GET32 wraps the identical
      ; expression the identical way.  This is the ONLY push in this
      ; file that can exceed 31 bits; every other one is a byte, a
      ; small count, a verdict code, 0 or -1.
      T0Push(T0Norm(Xr32(a)))
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; read8-low, read-blob-inner, fail, zero-server-name, dn-hash-length.
Procedure XnIo(nid.i)
  Protected b.i
  Protected addr.i
  Protected len.i
  Protected clen.i
  Protected i.i
  Select nid
    Case #T0N_read8_low
      If xHlen = 0
        T0Push(-1)
      Else
        b = PeekA(xHbuf) & 255
        xHbuf = xHbuf + 1
        xHlen = xHlen - 1
        XfeedByte(b)
        T0Push(b)
      EndIf
    Case #T0N_read_blob_inner
      len = T0Pop()
      addr = T0Pop()
      clen = xHlen
      If clen > len
        clen = len
      EndIf
      If addr <> 0
        ; A blob destination is as stack-supplied as a set8 offset, and
        ; its length comes off the same stack. Range the WHOLE write.
        If XrInRange(addr, clen) = 0
          ProcedureReturn
        EndIf
        i = 0
        While i < clen
          xRam[addr + i] = PeekA(xHbuf + i) & 255
          i = i + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
      XfeedChunk(xHbuf, clen)
      xHbuf = xHbuf + clen
      xHlen = xHlen - clen
      T0Push(addr + clen)
      T0Push(len - clen)
    Case #T0N_fail
      xErr = T0Pop()
    Case #T0N_zero_server_name
      If xServerLen < 0
        T0Push(-1)
      Else
        T0Push(0)
      EndIf
    Case #T0N_dn_hash_length
      T0Push(#X_DNHASH_LEN)
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; start/stop-tbs-hash, start/compute-dn-hash, compute-tbs-hash.
Procedure XnHash(nid.i)
  Protected id.i
  Select nid
    Case #T0N_start_tbs_hash
      Sha256Begin()
      Sha256Snapshot(@xTbsSnap)
      xDoMhash = 1
    Case #T0N_stop_tbs_hash
      xDoMhash = 0
    Case #T0N_start_dn_hash
      Sha256Begin()
      Sha256Snapshot(@xDnSnap)
      xDoDnhash = 1
    Case #T0N_compute_dn_hash
      Sha256Restore(@xDnSnap)
      Sha256End(@xRam[#X_current_dn_hash])
      xDoDnhash = 0
    Case #T0N_compute_tbs_hash
      id = T0Pop()
      If id = 4                        ; only SHA-256 is wired
        Sha256Restore(@xTbsSnap)
        Sha256End(@xTbsHash)
        T0Push(32)
      Else
        T0Push(0)                      ; unsupported hash -> engine faults loudly
      EndIf
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; blobcopy, eqOID, eqblob, offset-name-element, copy-name-element,
; copy-name-SAN. Name gathering is not configured in this build, so the
; three name natives operate on an empty element set (faithful no-ops).
Procedure XnBlob(nid.i)
  Protected len.i
  Protected src.i
  Protected dst.i
  Protected i.i
  Protected oidAddr.i
  Protected padLen.i
  Protected r.i
  Select nid
    Case #T0N_blobcopy
      len = T0Pop()
      src = T0Pop()
      dst = T0Pop()
      ; Three stack values, two of them offsets into the array. Range both
      ; over the full length before a byte moves.
      If XrInRange(dst, len) = 0
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      If XrInRange(src, len) = 0
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      i = 0
      While i < len
        xRam[dst + i] = xRam[src + i]
        i = i + 1
      Wend
    Case #T0N_eqOID
      oidAddr = t0_datap + T0Pop()
      padLen = xRam[#X_pad] & 255
      r = 0
      If padLen = (PeekA(oidAddr) & 255)
        r = -1
        i = 0
        While i < padLen
          If (xRam[#X_pad + 1 + i] & 255) <> (PeekA(oidAddr + 1 + i) & 255)
            r = 0
            Break
          EndIf
          i = i + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
      T0Push(r)
    Case #T0N_eqblob
      len = T0Pop()
      src = T0Pop()                    ; addr2
      dst = T0Pop()                    ; addr1
      If XramEq(dst, src, len)
        T0Push(-1)
      Else
        T0Push(0)
      EndIf
    Case #T0N_offset_name_element
      T0Pop()                          ; san flag - no elements configured
      T0Push(-1)
    Case #T0N_copy_name_element
      T0Pop()                          ; offbuf
      T0Pop()                          ; ok
    Case #T0N_copy_name_SAN
      T0Pop()                          ; tag
      T0Pop()                          ; ok
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; check-validity-range, match-server-name.
Procedure XnCheck(nid.i)
  Protected nbs.i
  Protected nbd.i
  Protected nas.i
  Protected nad.i
  Protected r.i
  Protected n1.i
  Protected n2.i
  Protected i.i
  Protected u.i
  Protected c1.i
  Protected c2.i
  Protected ok.i
  Select nid
    Case #T0N_check_validity_range
      nbs = T0Pop()
      nbd = T0Pop()
      nas = T0Pop()
      nad = T0Pop()
      If xValidDays < nbd Or (xValidDays = nbd And xValidSecs < nbs)
        r = -1
      ElseIf xValidDays > nad Or (xValidDays = nad And xValidSecs > nas)
        r = 1
      Else
        r = 0
      EndIf
      T0Push(r)
    Case #T0N_match_server_name
      If xServerLen < 0
        T0Push(0)
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      n1 = xServerLen
      n2 = xRam[#X_pad] & 255
      ok = 0
      If n1 = n2
        ok = 1
        i = 0
        While i < n1
          c1 = xRam[#X_pad + 1 + i] & 255
          c2 = PeekA(xServerAddr + i) & 255
          If c1 >= 65 And c1 <= 90
            c1 = c1 + 32
          EndIf
          If c2 >= 65 And c2 <= 90
            c2 = c2 + 32
          EndIf
          If c1 <> c2
            ok = 0
            Break
          EndIf
          i = i + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
      If ok = 0 And n2 >= 2 And (xRam[#X_pad + 1] & 255) = 42 And (xRam[#X_pad + 2] & 255) = 46
        ; wildcard "*." : match the tail after the first label of the name
        u = 0
        While u < n1 And (PeekA(xServerAddr + u) & 255) <> 46
          u = u + 1
        Wend
        u = u + 1
        n1 = n1 - u
        If (n2 - 2) = n1
          ok = 1
          i = 0
          While i < n1
            c1 = xRam[#X_pad + 3 + i] & 255
            c2 = PeekA(xServerAddr + u + i) & 255
            If c1 >= 65 And c1 <= 90
              c1 = c1 + 32
            EndIf
            If c2 >= 65 And c2 <= 90
              c2 = c2 + 32
            EndIf
            If c1 <> c2
              ok = 0
              Break
            EndIf
            i = i + 1
          Wend
        EndIf
      EndIf
      If ok
        T0Push(-1)
      Else
        T0Push(0)
      EndIf
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; Big-integer equality over two host buffers, ignoring leading zero bytes
; (unsigned big-endian, as RSA moduli/exponents are stored).
Procedure.i XeqBigHost(p1.i, l1.i, p2.i, l2.i)
  Protected i.i
  While l1 > 0 And (PeekA(p1) & 255) = 0
    p1 = p1 + 1
    l1 = l1 - 1
  Wend
  While l2 > 0 And (PeekA(p2) & 255) = 0
    p2 = p2 + 1
    l2 = l2 - 1
  Wend
  If l1 <> l2
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  i = 0
  While i < l1
    If (PeekA(p1 + i) & 255) <> (PeekA(p2 + i) & 255)
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; copy-ee-rsa-pkey, copy-ee-ec-pkey, check-direct-trust,
; check-trust-anchor-CA, do-rsa-vrfy, do-ecdsa-vrfy.
Procedure XnKey(nid.i)
  Protected nlen.i
  Protected elen.i
  Protected qlen.i
  Protected curve.i
  Protected u.i
  Protected i.i
  Protected kt.i
  Protected match.i
  Select nid
    Case #T0N_copy_ee_rsa_pkey
      elen = T0Pop()
      nlen = T0Pop()
      ; Both lengths came off the data stack, so both are certificate
      ; bytes, and together they size a write into a fixed buffer.
      If XkeyFits(nlen, elen) = 0
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      i = 0
      While i < nlen + elen
        xEePkey[i] = xRam[#X_pkey_data + i]
        i = i + 1
      Wend
      xPkeyType = 1
      X509EePkeyType = 1
      X509EeNAddr = @xEePkey[0]
      X509EeNLen = nlen
      X509EeEAddr = @xEePkey[nlen]
      X509EeELen = elen
    Case #T0N_copy_ee_ec_pkey
      qlen = T0Pop()
      curve = T0Pop()
      ; One length here, and the same rule: it is a stack value sizing a
      ; write into the same fixed buffer.
      If XkeyFits(qlen, 0) = 0
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      i = 0
      While i < qlen
        xEePkey[i] = xRam[#X_pkey_data + i]
        i = i + 1
      Wend
      xPkeyType = 2
      X509EePkeyType = 2
      X509EeCurve = curve
      X509EeQAddr = @xEePkey[0]
      X509EeQLen = qlen
    Case #T0N_check_direct_trust
      u = 0
      While u < xNumAnchors
        If xAncCA[u] = 0
          ; hash the anchor DN and compare with the EE subject DN hash
          Sha256Of(xAncDnAddr[u], xAncDnLen[u], @xTmpHash)
          match = 1
          i = 0
          While i < #X_DNHASH_LEN
            If (xTmpHash[i] & 255) <> (xRam[#X_current_dn_hash + i] & 255)
              match = 0
              Break
            EndIf
            i = i + 1
          Wend
          If match <> 0 And xAncType[u] = xPkeyType
            If xPkeyType = 1
              If XeqBigHost(X509EeNAddr, X509EeNLen, xAncNAddr[u], xAncNLen[u]) <> 0 And XeqBigHost(X509EeEAddr, X509EeELen, xAncEAddr[u], xAncELen[u]) <> 0
                xErr = #X509ERR_OK
                ProcedureReturn
              EndIf
            ElseIf xPkeyType = 2
              If X509EeCurve = xAncCurve[u] And X509EeQLen = xAncQLen[u]
                match = 1
                i = 0
                While i < X509EeQLen
                  If (PeekA(X509EeQAddr + i) & 255) <> (PeekA(xAncQAddr[u] + i) & 255)
                    match = 0
                    Break
                  EndIf
                  i = i + 1
                Wend
                If match
                  xErr = #X509ERR_OK
                  ProcedureReturn
                EndIf
              EndIf
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
        u = u + 1
      Wend
    Case #T0N_check_trust_anchor_CA
      u = 0
      While u < xNumAnchors
        If xAncCA[u] <> 0
          Sha256Of(xAncDnAddr[u], xAncDnLen[u], @xTmpHash)
          match = 1
          i = 0
          While i < #X_DNHASH_LEN
            If (xTmpHash[i] & 255) <> (xRam[#X_saved_dn_hash + i] & 255)
              match = 0
              Break
            EndIf
            i = i + 1
          Wend
          If match
            If xAncType[u] = 1
              If X509VerifySig(1, xAncNAddr[u], xAncNLen[u], xAncEAddr[u], xAncELen[u]) = 0
                xErr = #X509ERR_OK
                ProcedureReturn
              EndIf
            ElseIf xAncType[u] = 2
              If X509VerifySig(2, xAncCurve[u], 0, xAncQAddr[u], xAncQLen[u]) = 0
                xErr = #X509ERR_OK
                ProcedureReturn
              EndIf
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
        u = u + 1
      Wend
    Case #T0N_do_rsa_vrfy
      elen = T0Pop()
      nlen = T0Pop()
      ; key just decoded into pkey_data: n at pkey_data, e at pkey_data+nlen
      ; Same two stack lengths as the copy above, and here they become a
      ; raw ADDRESS handed to the verifier - a wild length would send it
      ; off the end of the array with no way to know. Ranged first.
      If XkeyFits(nlen, elen) = 0
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      T0Push(X509VerifySig(1, @xRam[#X_pkey_data], nlen, @xRam[#X_pkey_data + nlen], elen))
    Case #T0N_do_ecdsa_vrfy
      qlen = T0Pop()
      curve = T0Pop()
      If XkeyFits(qlen, 0) = 0
        ProcedureReturn
      EndIf
      T0Push(X509VerifySig(2, curve, 0, @xRam[#X_pkey_data], qlen))
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Native dispatcher: route each native id to its group.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure X509Native(nid.i)
  If nid = #T0N_set8 Or nid = #T0N_set16 Or nid = #T0N_set32 Or nid = #T0N_get16 Or nid = #T0N_get32
    XnMem(nid)
  ElseIf nid = #T0N_read8_low Or nid = #T0N_read_blob_inner Or nid = #T0N_fail Or nid = #T0N_zero_server_name Or nid = #T0N_dn_hash_length
    XnIo(nid)
  ElseIf nid = #T0N_start_tbs_hash Or nid = #T0N_stop_tbs_hash Or nid = #T0N_start_dn_hash Or nid = #T0N_compute_dn_hash Or nid = #T0N_compute_tbs_hash
    XnHash(nid)
  ElseIf nid = #T0N_blobcopy Or nid = #T0N_eqOID Or nid = #T0N_eqblob Or nid = #T0N_offset_name_element Or nid = #T0N_copy_name_element Or nid = #T0N_copy_name_SAN
    XnBlob(nid)
  ElseIf nid = #T0N_check_validity_range Or nid = #T0N_match_server_name
    XnCheck(nid)
  ElseIf nid = #T0N_copy_ee_rsa_pkey Or nid = #T0N_copy_ee_ec_pkey Or nid = #T0N_check_direct_trust Or nid = #T0N_check_trust_anchor_CA Or nid = #T0N_do_rsa_vrfy Or nid = #T0N_do_ecdsa_vrfy
    XnKey(nid)
  Else
    xErr = #X509ERR_GUARD
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Trust-anchor configuration.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure X509ResetAnchors()
  xNumAnchors = 0
EndProcedure

Procedure X509AddAnchorRSA(isCA.i, dnAddr.i, dnLen.i, nAddr.i, nLen.i, eAddr.i, eLen.i)
  Protected u.i
  If xNumAnchors >= #X_MAX_ANCHORS
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  u = xNumAnchors
  xAncCA[u] = isCA
  xAncDnAddr[u] = dnAddr
  xAncDnLen[u] = dnLen
  xAncType[u] = 1
  xAncNAddr[u] = nAddr
  xAncNLen[u] = nLen
  xAncEAddr[u] = eAddr
  xAncELen[u] = eLen
  xNumAnchors = u + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure X509AddAnchorEC(isCA.i, dnAddr.i, dnLen.i, curve.i, qAddr.i, qLen.i)
  Protected u.i
  If xNumAnchors >= #X_MAX_ANCHORS
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  u = xNumAnchors
  xAncCA[u] = isCA
  xAncDnAddr[u] = dnAddr
  xAncDnLen[u] = dnLen
  xAncType[u] = 2
  xAncCurve[u] = curve
  xAncQAddr[u] = qAddr
  xAncQLen[u] = qLen
  xNumAnchors = u + 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The driver. Feeds one cert through the running coroutine, servicing
;  native words, until the cert is consumed (a benign yield with the input
;  exhausted) or the engine reaches a terminal state (err set).
;  Returns 1 if terminal (X509 err set), 0 if the cert was consumed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i X509RunOneCert()
  Protected st.i
  Protected guard.i
  guard = 0
  Repeat
    st = T0Run()
    If st = #T0_NATIVE
      X509Native(t0_native_id)
      If xErr <> 0
        ProcedureReturn 1
      EndIf
    ElseIf st = #T0_YIELD
      If xErr <> 0
        ProcedureReturn 1
      EndIf
      If xHlen <= 0
        ProcedureReturn 0                 ; cert boundary - ready for next
      EndIf
    Else
      ProcedureReturn 1                    ; HALT (unexpected for this engine)
    EndIf
    guard = guard + 1
    If guard > 20000000
      xErr = #X509ERR_GUARD
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  X509ValidateChain - the public entry. See the header for the contract.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i X509ValidateChain(certsAddr.i, lensAddr.i, count.i, serverAddr.i, serverLen.i, unixTime.i)
  Protected i.i
  Protected certAddr.i
  Protected certLen.i
  Protected terminal.i

  ; reset the whole context RAM and run state
  i = 0
  While i < #X_RAM_SIZE
    xRam[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  xErr = 0
  X509Err = 0
  xDoMhash = 0
  xDoDnhash = 0
  xPkeyType = 0
  X509EePkeyType = 0
  xServerAddr = serverAddr
  xServerLen = serverLen

  ; date, in the day/second epoch the engine's date decoder produces.
  ; MEASURED DIFFERENCE, NOT A DEFECT: a unixTime past 2038 fits here
  ; and does not fit a 32-bit `.i`.  The shared gate's "expired" case
  ; passes 4070908800 (2099-01-01); on the Picos that arrives negative
  ; and the case is refused by the notBefore side of
  ; check-validity-range, while here it is refused by the notAfter
  ; side - the one the case is named after.  Same verdict (54), and on
  ; this part the two date cases really do cover both branches.
  xValidDays = (unixTime / 86400) + 719528
  xValidSecs = unixTime % 86400

  If count <= 0
    X509Err = #X509ERR_EMPTY_CHAIN
    ProcedureReturn X509Err
  EndIf

  ; enter the engine at its main word
  T0LoadX509()
  T0Start()

  i = 0
  While i < count
    ; A CELL IS EIGHT BYTES HERE AND PeekI READS EIGHT.  The 32-bit
    ; copies say `i * 4` because on them a cell is four and PeekI
    ; reads four; on this part BOTH halves of that line were wrong,
    ; and separately.  Element 0 still came back right, so a
    ; one-certificate chain validated and looked like proof; from
    ; element 1 the read straddled the high half of one cell and the
    ; low half of the next and the issuer certificate was garbage.
    certAddr = PeekI(certsAddr + i * #X_CELL)
    certLen  = PeekI(lensAddr + i * #X_CELL)
    ; mirror start_cert: set the advertised certificate length
    XrSet32(#X_cert_length, certLen)
    ; mirror append: hand over the bytes
    xHbuf = certAddr
    xHlen = certLen
    terminal = X509RunOneCert()
    If terminal
      Break
    EndIf
    ; mirror end_cert: count the processed certificate
    XrSet32(#X_num_certs, Xr32(#X_num_certs) + 1)
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  ; mirror end_chain
  If xErr = #X509ERR_OK
    X509Err = #X509ERR_OK
  ElseIf xErr <> 0
    X509Err = xErr
  Else
    X509Err = #X509ERR_NOT_TRUSTED
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn X509Err
EndProcedure
; ==== PORTABLE CORE A64 END ====
